---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import workspace from '../../assets/projects/waypoint-detail.jpg';
---

<Layout
  title="Waypoint"
  description="A personal careers tracker for organising employers, opportunities and applications, built with React, TanStack Start, Convex and WorkOS."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Waypoint"
      description="Keeping career opportunities and applications together."
      stack="React / TanStack Start / Convex / WorkOS"
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="https://waypoint.freddiephilpot.dev">
        Open app ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/waypoint">
        Source ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/waypoint/releases">
        Releases ↗
      </a>
    </CaseHeader>
    <figure class="case-image">
      <a
        class="case-image-link"
        href={workspace.src}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        aria-label="Open Waypoint screenshot at full size (new tab)"
      >
        <Image
          src={workspace}
          alt="Waypoint’s application list and record editor, rendered with illustrative example records"
          widths={[640, 1000, 1600]}
          sizes="90vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={95}
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        The existing application components, captured locally with illustrative
        records. No private career data is shown. Select the image to enlarge.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Something I needed</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I started Waypoint to organise my own career research: employers to
          look into, opportunities worth following and applications already in
          progress. I wanted the links, notes and deadlines to stay connected,
          with a clear next action when I returned to them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is a private careers workspace. It separates researching an
          opportunity from applying for it, while keeping both attached to the
          relevant employer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Following an application</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Applications have stages, deadlines, notes, links and a next action.
          Board and list views provide different ways to review progress, while
          checklists and stage history retain the detail behind each
          application.
        </p>
        <p>
          Company and opportunity records hold research separately, including
          locations and relevant links. Search and filters help narrow the
          workspace. I have focused on making the next step easy to find without
          needing to open every record.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Data & authentication</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          The web app uses React and TanStack Start, with Convex for data and
          WorkOS AuthKit for sign-in. Companies, opportunities and applications
          are separate tables linked by IDs. An application can refer to a
          specific opportunity or directly to an employer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Convex functions verify the authenticated user and check ownership of
          related records. They also check that a selected opportunity belongs
          to the chosen company. Keeping these rules on the server matters even
          when the interface already filters the available choices.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/waypoint/blob/main/convex/applications.ts"
        >
          Application data functions ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Restoring a session</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          A real issue was a login that could succeed initially but fail to
          restore reliably on the public hostname. The authentication
          investigation found a mismatch between the browser session approach
          and the deployment, as well as a separate token-refresh problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          The web implementation moved to WorkOS’s TanStack Start SDK with an
          encrypted, HTTP-only session cookie. Refresh tokens stay with the
          server session, and the Convex bridge forwards forced-refresh
          requests. Local production-build checks covered reloads, direct
          navigation, another tab and sign-out.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those local checks do not establish that the public deployment has the
          matching configuration. The public sign-in page is available, but a
          full authenticated production session still needs separate
          verification.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/waypoint/blob/main/docs/web-authentication.md"
        >
          Authentication investigation ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>What I’m learning</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Working on Waypoint has helped me connect database relationships with
          everyday usability. Separating an employer, an opportunity and an
          application makes the data clearer, but the interface still needs to
          make moving between them straightforward.
        </p>
        <p>
          It has also shown me why a visible signed-in interface is only part of
          authentication: the backend needs to accept the session, restore it
          consistently and enforce ownership on every operation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <a class="next-project" href="/projects/glyph">
      <span>Next project</span>
      <strong>
        Glyph <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </strong>
    </a>
  </article>
</Layout>
